## Why This Is Asked
Tests a practical, auditable CAPA design for IaC drift and nonconforming changes in a multi-tenant setup, plus concrete API, lifecycle, and metrics.

## Key Concepts
- CAPA data model
- Lifecycle state machine
- Evidence capture and auditability
- IaC drift detection and canary remediation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal API sketch for POST /caps
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());
app.post('/caps', (req, res) => {
  // store CAPA with req.body evidence
  res.status(201).send({ id: 'CAPA-123', ...req.body });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for audit logging and compliance needs?
- How would you prioritize CAPAs with conflicting remediation actions?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model CAPA data, implement region-aware governance, and handle third-party feed drift with a real-world workflow, including evidence lineage, owners, and canary rollouts.

## Key Concepts

- CAPA data model: incidentTime, vendor, feed, evidenceLinks, lineage, owners, actions, status, region
- Lifecycle: detect -> contain -> triage -> diagnose -> remediate -> verify -> rollback with region gates
- APIs: minimal CRUD (POST /caps, PUT /caps/{id}) including logs/traces and lineage
- Metrics: containment time by region/vendor, recurrence rate, false positives
- RCA & canary: structured root-cause sections; schema validation; provider failover

## Code Example

```typescript
type CAPA = {
  id: string;
  incidentTime: string;
  vendor: string;
  feed: string;
  evidenceLinks: string[];
  lineage: string;
  owners: string[];
  actions: string[];
  status: 'detect'|'contain'|'triage'|'diagnose'|'remediate'|'verify'|'rollback'|'closed';
  region: string;
}
```

